  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 639 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nActive weather continues into the long term period, with daily \nchances for showers and storms in the forecast grids. Expect the \ngreatest precipitation coverage during the first half of the \nforecast period, when a more favorable troughing pattern will be in \nplace over the Ohio River Valley and Greater Midwest. A series of \nshortwave impulses moving through this broader troughing will \nbolster late-week rain chances across the commonwealth, and recent \nforecast guidance suggests that a better-defined disturbance will \ndig into the commonwealth in the Friday to Saturday time frame. The \nresultant increase in forcing could allow more locations to \nexperience measurable rainfall on these dates. Likewise, relatively \nhigher amounts of shear could allow those week-end showers/storms to \norganize beyond the typical summertime pop-up archetype. However, \nthe intensity of any late-week activity is largely conditional upon \nthat parent disturbance aligning with the diurnal instability cycle. \nSuch timing details remain highly uncertain, and there is not enough \nforecast confidence to delineate any severe weather risks in this \ntime frame. \n\nThere is much higher confidence in the forecast for multiple rounds \nof heavy rainfall across portions of Eastern Kentucky through the\nend of the week. Highs in the mid to upper 80s and dewpoints in \nthe 70s will pose favorable thermodynamics for convective lift on \nThursday, Friday, and Saturday afternoon. Ensemble data continues \nto resolve 1500-2000+ J/kg of CAPE across Eastern Kentucky during \npeak diurnal heating. Mean soundings around the region depict very\nlittle CIN alongside high freezing levels (above 15k feet) and \ncloud-layer winds in the 10-15 knot range. Given all of the above \nand PWATs in the 1.7-1.9\" range, the thunderstorms that develop \nlater this week are poised to be slow-moving and efficient \nrainmakers. Even if the previously-discussed shortwave \ndisturbances arrive at night and storms are unable to take \nadvantage of peak diurnal instability, the repetitive rainfall \ncould prove excessive. Isolated instances of flash flooding cannot\nbe ruled out if any given location experiences multiple downpours\nin the same day or if slow-moving storms track over the same \nplaces multiple days in a row. To cover this risk, WPC has \ndelineated a series of Marginal (Level 1/4) Excessive Rainfall \nOutlooks across the entire JKL CWA for these three days. \n\nForecast confidence decreases next week, but this morning's forecast \nguidance continued the trend towards a drier early-week forecast. \nThe latest data collectively resolves midlevel height rises and weak \nquasi-zonal flow over the Ohio River Valley for Sunday, Monday, and \nTuesday. This is in response to the amplification of a ridge across \nthe Southwestern and Central CONUS, although there is still some \nuncertainty regarding its eastern extent. If a stronger ridge \nemerges, temperatures could bump up in our forecast area. For now \nthough, the forecast calls for climatologically appropriate \nthermometer readings. Expect highs in the mid/upper 80s and lows in \nthe 60s/near 70s, albeit with less moisture than earlier in the \nperiod. Ensemble mean PWATs decrease to between 1.4-1.6\" next week, \nlargely due to the advection of a drier continental airmass in the \nmidlevels. The late-period PoPs are largely modulated by the diurnal \nheating cycle. There could still be isolated to scattered summertime \npop-ups, but barring any ridge-riding shortwave support, the late-\nperiod showers and storms should be fairly mundane. In short, the \nforecast area is in store for typical summertime sensible weather \nnext week.",
